# Per-user runtime settings. tiko rewrites these as you use it, so tracking them means
# every run shows up as a diff and every pull risks a conflict over someone else's window
# positions and key bindings.
#
# Paths are anchored with a leading "/" on purpose: settings/keywords/*.ini ARE tracked
# (codetips.ini and friends), so a bare *.ini rule would wrongly catch those too.
/settings/keybindings.ini
/settings/settings.ini
# The untitled workspace: whatever loose files the user last had open. Rewritten on every
# exit, so it is runtime state exactly like the two above. (settings/default.session, which
# it replaces, was tracked -- which is why a pull could hand you someone else's open files.)
/settings/default.tiko

# Internal design / planning notes. NEVER published: this repo is public on GitHub
# and clients can browse it.
REFACTOR_PLAN*.md

# Env-gated theme diagnostics (TIKO_THEME_TRACE=1), and the self-test's scratch file.
/theme_dump.txt
/theme_trace.log
/_theme_selftest.tmp

# settings/help/helpcenter IS TRACKED, deliberately. It used to be ignored, on the grounds
# that the generated site is ~31,770 files / ~147 MB and every helpgen regeneration rewrites
# all of them (the page shell carries cache-busting ?v= hashes) -- so each regen adds ~31k
# blobs to history, permanently, since removing them later means a filter-repo force-push
# that breaks every clone. That cost is accepted: it is what makes a fresh clone have a
# working Help Center instead of one that reports its files as missing until
# _copy_helpcenter.bat is run by hand, and that script is no longer in the repo to run.
#
# Consequence to keep in mind: DO NOT commit a regeneration that only changed the ?v=
# hashes. Commit one when the DOCS changed.

# A note the removed rule earned: an ignore entry does nothing to files that are ALREADY
# TRACKED, and settings\helpcenter had been committed before that rule was written -- which
# is why moving the tree surfaced as a mass deletion rather than as nothing at all. Same
# trap as REFACTOR_PLAN.md. It only bites once, but check with
# `git ls-files --error-unmatch <path>` before trusting a rule like it.

# Bytecode beside settings\help\tikohelp\tools\build.py. The generator's SOURCE is tracked
# -- it is what regenerates that help site -- but its .pyc are per-interpreter and would
# churn on every Python upgrade.
__pycache__/

# Local staging helpers and point-in-time notes, untracked on request. The .bat files copy
# built DLLs / the generated Help Center in from their own repos under C:\dev, so they are
# only meaningful on a machine that has those trees; THEMES.md and CODE_REVIEW.md are
# snapshots of a tree that has moved on. Kept on disk, out of the public repo.
#
# NOTE: these rules do nothing on their own -- all six were ALREADY TRACKED, so they had to
# be `git rm --cached`d first. Same trap as REFACTOR_PLAN.md, noted above.
/_copy*.*
/THEMES.md
/CODE_REVIEW.md
_pscore_probe.exe
_check_app_layer.exe

# The app-standalone gate scratch files.
src/_standalone.*
src/_prelude.txt

# Phase 7c's shell binary and its output directory. UNLIKE tiko.exe, which is tracked: this
# one is a mid-conversion artifact on a branch that is un-shippable by construction and will
# never be merged in this state, so a committed copy would only ever be stale. It also has
# to stay OUT of the project root -- libpsscintilla.dll sits beside tiko.exe and Windows
# loads that copy in preference to anything on PATH, so two exes in one directory would
# share a shim that only one of them is rebuilt against. Build it with _compile_shell.bat,
# run it with _run_shell.bat.
/_shell/

# The pump oracle's output (modPumpTrace.inc, TIKO_PUMP_TRACE=1). It is a record of one
# SESSION -- what the author happened to do for a few minutes -- so it is generated, not
# source. The captures that a census actually cites are copied into docs/port/pump-census/
# deliberately and by hand; anything left lying in the root is the last run, not evidence.
/pump-trace.txt
